# "A Common Pride and Fame": The Attack and Repulse of Pickett's Division, July 3, 1863, Part II: the condition of the wall at the Angle in two accounts the study quotes (filed after 94-common-pride-1b.xdef; the text layer is a typescript with light OCR noise, quotes in the cleaned form).
@ NPS-COMMON-PRIDE-2

%basis Captain Andrew Cowan, 1st New York Independent Battery, as quoted by the study (the study's note names his canister fire at 'ten paces'; the letter is in the Alexander S. Webb Papers, Yale University)
82/299 | fences | At the Angle the wall was almost demolished, nowhere over a foot high in front of Cowan's guns; only the wall and some rails, as if thrown down to let artillery pass | P-01,E-17,FEN-cemetery-ridge-walls | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study quotes Captain Cowan's account that some of the rebels came on and crossed the wall, then almost demolished and nowhere over a foot high in front of his guns, and that his battery had no works, nothing but the wall and some rails, which looked as if they had been thrown down to let artillery pass beyond them (his other account, quoted in Part I of the study, says the wall was not over two feet high) | =Some of the Rebels came on and crossed the Wall (then almost demolished and nowhere over a foot high in my front). | =We had no works--nothing but the wall, and some rails, and that looked as if it had been thrown down to let Artillery pass beyond it.
%basis a regimental account of 1904 as quoted by the study
21/- | fences | On the left of the 69th Pennsylvania the stone wall had been broken down the day before for the passage of artillery; the Confederates poured through the gap | P-01,E-17,FEN-cemetery-ridge-walls | 1863-07-03 (a gap made on July 2) | stated | The study quotes a late regimental account that on the left of the regiment the stone wall had been broken down the day before for the passage of artillery and that the Confederates also poured through this gap | On the left of the regiment the stone wall had been broken down >>> through this gap the Confederates also poured | As late as 1904, members of the regiment were yet giving readers their own version of the battle
